{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1788507003913,"sessionID":"ses_f94b3b3fdffeGjmu6JJ70nAmp6","part":{"id":"prt_06b52cf330013Qip13tp5qe9ER","messageID":"msg_06b52bdb8001cBuQoh7ExZSFmc","sessionID":"ses_f94b3b3fdffeGjmu6JJ70nAmp6","type":"reasoning","text":"In 2.10.1, `api_jwt.py` no longer raises DecodeError for missing algorithms — the check was removed entirely and delegated to `api_jws.decode_complete` (line 204 has the PyJWK-aware check).\n\nSo the final upstream design: `api_jwt.decode_complete` has NO algorithms check; `api_jws.decode_complete` does the PyJWK-aware check. \n\nFor the testbed, the minimal change consistent with both the user's explicit requirement (\"work consistently across all decode operations, including both jwt.decode() and jwt.decode_complete()\") and the final upstream design is to make the JWT-level check PyJWK-aware rather than removing it entirely (removing it entirely would change behavior for non-PyJWK keys — the DecodeError for missing algorithms when key is not PyJWK must be preserved; it's a heavily tested behavior, e.g. test_api_jwt.py tests decode without algorithms raising DecodeError).\n\nWait — if I removed the check from api_jwt.py, api_jws.decode_complete would still raise DecodeError for non-PyJWK keys. So removing is also safe behavior-wise. But minimal diff: keep the check in api_jwt.py but add the PyJWK exception. Hmm, which is better?\n\nOption A (PyJWK-aware check in api_jwt):\n```python\nif options[\"verify_signature\"] and not algorithms and not isinstance(key, PyJWK):\n    raise DecodeError(...)\n```\nRequires runtime import of PyJWK in api_jwt.py.\n\nOption B (remove check from api_jwt entirely): relies on api_jws check. This is what upstream eventually did. But the testbed's api_jwt tests: are there tests that rely on the JWT-level DecodeError message? Let me check test_api_jwt.py line 92 area:\n\n```python\n        with pytest.raises(DecodeError):\n            jwt.decode(example_jwt, example_secret, algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\n```\n\nHmm that's with algorithms but no key? Let me look. Line 92-105: \"decoded_payload = jwt.decode(example_jwt, example_secret, algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\" at 45... Let me check for a test that decodes without algorithms.\n\nEither way, with Option B, `jwt.decode(token, \"secret\")` without algorithms → api_jws.decode_complete raises the same DecodeError message. Behavior preserved.\n\nWith Option A, the JWT-level check raises first for non-PyJWK; same message. Both fine.\n\nI'll go with Option A — it's the minimal, explicit change, and keeps the message logic in both layers consistent. Also matches the spirit of the upstream `_verify_signature` change. Actually wait — hmm, with Option A, `algorithms` passed to api_jws.decode_complete stays None for the PyJWK case, and api_jws handles it. Good.\n\nLet me now update api_jwt.py:\n\n1. Move `PyJWK` import from TYPE_CHECKING to runtime: `from .api_jwk import PyJWK` (after `from . import api_jws`). Actually to avoid a potential circular import: api_jwt imports api_jws, api_jws imports api_jwk. api_jwk imports algorithms/exceptions/types. No cycle back to api_jwt. Safe.\n\nBut the TYPE_CHECKING import of PyJWK I added earlier — replace with runtime import. Then the type annotation can use the runtime class directly. Keep TYPE_CHECKING for AllowedPrivateKeys/AllowedPublicKeys as-is.\n\nLet me make the edit.","time":{"start":1788506984243,"end":1788507003881},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"In 2.10.1, `api_jwt.py` no longer raises DecodeError for missing algorithms — the check was removed entirely and delegated to `api_jws.decode_complete` (line 204 has the PyJWK-aware check).\n\nSo the final upstream design: `api_jwt.decode_complete` has NO algorithms check; `api_jws.decode_complete` does the PyJWK-aware check. \n\nFor the testbed, the minimal change consistent with both the user's explicit requirement (\"work consistently across all decode operations, including both jwt.decode() and jwt.decode_complete()\") and the final upstream design is to make the JWT-level check PyJWK-aware rather than removing it entirely (removing it entirely would change behavior for non-PyJWK keys — the DecodeError for missing algorithms when key is not PyJWK must be preserved; it's a heavily tested behavior, e.g. test_api_jwt.py tests decode without algorithms raising DecodeError).\n\nWait — if I removed the check from api_jwt.py, api_jws.decode_complete would still raise DecodeError for non-PyJWK keys. So removing is also safe behavior-wise. But minimal diff: keep the check in api_jwt.py but add the PyJWK exception. Hmm, which is better?\n\nOption A (PyJWK-aware check in api_jwt):\n```python\nif options[\"verify_signature\"] and not algorithms and not isinstance(key, PyJWK):\n    raise DecodeError(...)\n```\nRequires runtime import of PyJWK in api_jwt.py.\n\nOption B (remove check from api_jwt entirely): relies on api_jws check. This is what upstream eventually did. But the testbed's api_jwt tests: are there tests that rely on the JWT-level DecodeError message? Let me check test_api_jwt.py line 92 area:\n\n```python\n        with pytest.raises(DecodeError):\n            jwt.decode(example_jwt, example_secret, algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\n```\n\nHmm that's with algorithms but no key? Let me look. Line 92-105: \"decoded_payload = jwt.decode(example_jwt, example_secret, algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\" at 45... Let me check for a test that decodes without algorithms.\n\nEither way, with Option B, `jwt.decode(token, \"secret\")` without algorithms → api_jws.decode_complete raises the same DecodeError message. Behavior preserved.\n\nWith Option A, the JWT-level check raises first for non-PyJWK; same message. Both fine.\n\nI'll go with Option A — it's the minimal, explicit change, and keeps the message logic in both layers consistent. Also matches the spirit of the upstream `_verify_signature` change. Actually wait — hmm, with Option A, `algorithms` passed to api_jws.decode_complete stays None for the PyJWK case, and api_jws handles it. Good.\n\nLet me now update api_jwt.py:\n\n1. Move `PyJWK` import from TYPE_CHECKING to runtime: `from .api_jwk import PyJWK` (after `from . import api_jws`). Actually to avoid a potential circular import: api_jwt imports api_jws, api_jws imports api_jwk. api_jwk imports algorithms/exceptions/types. No cycle back to api_jwt. Safe.\n\nBut the TYPE_CHECKING import of PyJWK I added earlier — replace with runtime import. Then the type annotation can use the runtime class directly. Keep TYPE_CHECKING for AllowedPrivateKeys/AllowedPublicKeys as-is.\n\nLet me make the edit.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788507003913,"sessionID":"ses_f94b3b3fdffeGjmu6JJ70nAmp6","part":{"id":"prt_06b531bec001Xel3Q9TVbFDfNJ","messageID":"msg_06b52bdb8001cBuQoh7ExZSFmc","sessionID":"ses_f94b3b3fdffeGjmu6JJ70nAmp6","type":"text","text":"Upstream's final design delegates the JWT-level check to `api_jws` (PyJWK-aware). I'll make the JWT-level check in `api_jwt.py` PyJWK-aware so `jwt.decode()`/`jwt.decode_complete()` work with PyJWK as required:","time":{"start":1788507003884,"end":1788507003909}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788507003949,"sessionID":"ses_f94b3b3fdffeGjmu6JJ70nAmp6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_3edc114577094531a4328808","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/testbed/jwt/api_jwt.py","limit":20,"offset":8},"output":"<path>/testbed/jwt/api_jwt.py</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n8: from typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Any, List\n9: \n10: from . import api_jws\n11: from .exceptions import (\n12:     DecodeError,\n13:     ExpiredSignatureError,\n14:     ImmatureSignatureError,\n15:     InvalidAudienceError,\n16:     InvalidIssuedAtError,\n17:     InvalidIssuerError,\n18:     MissingRequiredClaimError,\n19: )\n20: from .warnings import RemovedInPyjwt3Warning\n21: \n22: if TYPE_CHECKING:\n23:     from .algorithms import AllowedPrivateKeys, AllowedPublicKeys\n24:     from .api_jwk import PyJWK\n25: \n26: \n27: class PyJWT:\n\n(Showing lines 8-27 of 377. Use offset=28 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"from typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Any, List\n\nfrom . import api_jws\nfrom .exceptions import (\n    DecodeError,\n    ExpiredSignatureError,\n    ImmatureSignatureError,\n    InvalidAudienceError,\n    InvalidIssuedAtError,\n    InvalidIssuerError,\n    MissingRequiredClaimError,\n)\nfrom .warnings import RemovedInPyjwt3Warning\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .algorithms import AllowedPrivateKeys, AllowedPublicKeys\n    from .api_jwk import PyJWK\n\n\nclass PyJWT:","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/testbed/jwt/api_jwt.py","text":"from typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Any, List\n\nfrom . import api_jws\nfrom .exceptions import (\n    DecodeError,\n    ExpiredSignatureError,\n    ImmatureSignatureError,\n    InvalidAudienceError,\n    InvalidIssuedAtError,\n    InvalidIssuerError,\n    MissingRequiredClaimError,\n)\nfrom .warnings import RemovedInPyjwt3Warning\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .algorithms import AllowedPrivateKeys, AllowedPublicKeys\n    from .api_jwk import PyJWK\n\n\nclass PyJWT:","lineStart":8,"lineEnd":27,"totalLines":377,"truncated":true}},"title":"jwt/api_jwt.py","time":{"start":1788507003906,"end":1788507003942}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"In 2.10.1, `api_jwt.py` no longer raises DecodeError for missing algorithms — the check was removed entirely and delegated to `api_jws.decode_complete` (line 204 has the PyJWK-aware check).\n\nSo the final upstream design: `api_jwt.decode_complete` has NO algorithms check; `api_jws.decode_complete` does the PyJWK-aware check. \n\nFor the testbed, the minimal change consistent with both the user's explicit requirement (\"work consistently across all decode operations, including both jwt.decode() and jwt.decode_complete()\") and the final upstream design is to make the JWT-level check PyJWK-aware rather than removing it entirely (removing it entirely would change behavior for non-PyJWK keys — the DecodeError for missing algorithms when key is not PyJWK must be preserved; it's a heavily tested behavior, e.g. test_api_jwt.py tests decode without algorithms raising DecodeError).\n\nWait — if I removed the check from api_jwt.py, api_jws.decode_complete would still raise DecodeError for non-PyJWK keys. So removing is also safe behavior-wise. But minimal diff: keep the check in api_jwt.py but add the PyJWK exception. Hmm, which is better?\n\nOption A (PyJWK-aware check in api_jwt):\n```python\nif options[\"verify_signature\"] and not algorithms and not isinstance(key, PyJWK):\n    raise DecodeError(...)\n```\nRequires runtime import of PyJWK in api_jwt.py.\n\nOption B (remove check from api_jwt entirely): relies on api_jws check. This is what upstream eventually did. But the testbed's api_jwt tests: are there tests that rely on the JWT-level DecodeError message? Let me check test_api_jwt.py line 92 area:\n\n```python\n        with pytest.raises(DecodeError):\n            jwt.decode(example_jwt, example_secret, algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\n```\n\nHmm that's with algorithms but no key? Let me look. Line 92-105: \"decoded_payload = jwt.decode(example_jwt, example_secret, algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\" at 45... Let me check for a test that decodes without algorithms.\n\nEither way, with Option B, `jwt.decode(token, \"secret\")` without algorithms → api_jws.decode_complete raises the same DecodeError message. Behavior preserved.\n\nWith Option A, the JWT-level check raises first for non-PyJWK; same message. Both fine.\n\nI'll go with Option A — it's the minimal, explicit change, and keeps the message logic in both layers consistent. Also matches the spirit of the upstream `_verify_signature` change. Actually wait — hmm, with Option A, `algorithms` passed to api_jws.decode_complete stays None for the PyJWK case, and api_jws handles it. Good.\n\nLet me now update api_jwt.py:\n\n1. Move `PyJWK` import from TYPE_CHECKING to runtime: `from .api_jwk import PyJWK` (after `from . import api_jws`). Actually to avoid a potential circular import: api_jwt imports api_jws, api_jws imports api_jwk. api_jwk imports algorithms/exceptions/types. No cycle back to api_jwt. Safe.\n\nBut the TYPE_CHECKING import of PyJWK I added earlier — replace with runtime import. Then the type annotation can use the runtime class directly. Keep TYPE_CHECKING for AllowedPrivateKeys/AllowedPublicKeys as-is.\n\nLet me make the edit.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06b531bfc001Pi7lTVnUgflGyB","sessionID":"ses_f94b3b3fdffeGjmu6JJ70nAmp6","messageID":"msg_06b52bdb8001cBuQoh7ExZSFmc"}}
