{"type":"text","timestamp":1788507160053,"sessionID":"ses_f94b3b3fdffeGjmu6JJ70nAmp6","part":{"id":"prt_06b555d860012DqN2oli1FrMOs","messageID":"msg_06b554bcc001mFC6BRaF2cZEh3","sessionID":"ses_f94b3b3fdffeGjmu6JJ70nAmp6","type":"text","text":"All three improvements are implemented (mirroring upstream PyJWT PR #886, released in 2.9.0):\n\n1. **`jwt/api_jwk.py:55`** — `PyJWK` now stores `algorithm_name` (the resolved algorithm, whether from the JWK's `alg` field or derived from `kty`/`crv`).\n\n2. **`jwt/api_jws.py`, `jwt/api_jwt.py`** — `decode()` and `decode_complete()` accept a `PyJWK` as `key`. In `_verify_signature` (jwt/api_jws.py:379), when `algorithms` isn't given, it defaults to `[key.algorithm_name]`, and verification uses the JWK's own algorithm object and key — so a JWK for HS512 will reject a token signed with HS256 (`InvalidAlgorithmError`) instead of silently trying to verify with the wrong algorithm.\n\n3. **JWT-level support** — `jwt.decode()`/`jwt.decode_complete()` (PyJWT class) also pass PyJWK through; the \"algorithms required\" check (jwt/api_jwt.py:147) is skipped for PyJWK keys, while still raising `DecodeError` for plain string/bytes keys as before.\n\nAlso updated: docs examples now show `jwt.decode(token, signing_key, ...)` without `algorithms=[\"RS256\"]`, tests for `algorithm_name` and decode-with-PyJWK added, and CHANGELOG entries added.\n\nVerification: 264 tests pass (2 crypto-dependent skips, 1 xfail pre-existing); flake8, isort, black, and mypy are clean (the only black/mypy complaints are pre-existing in untouched files).","time":{"start":1788507151750,"end":1788507160046}}}
